://wikidata.org/entity/P27915500332cz  a2200145n  450000100060000000300040000600500170001000800410002704000080006810000330007640000180010940000220012740000220014994200150017139705DLC20250218094213.0800921n| azannaabn          |a aaa        aDLC1 aDel Litto, Victord1911-20041 aDel Litto, V.1 aLitto, Victor del1 aLitto, Victor del  aPERSO_NAME00352nz  a2200133n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400700006704000070013710000180014451000410016294200150020339706OI20260317121803.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1https://orcid.org/0009-0004-0505-37742ORCIDa0009-0004-0505-3774  aOI  aYu, Chonglian  aMusic, Thought and Technology926934  aPERSO_NAME00472nz  a2200145n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400880006704000070015510000350016294200150019755000480021255100660026039707OI20260330112619.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20302792WikidataaQ20302799Ilja Leonard Pfeijffer  aOI  aPfeijffer, Ilja Leonardd1968-  aPERSO_NAME  ihttp://wikidata.org/entity/P10692954aPoet  921879ihttp://wikidata.org/entity/P19aRijswijk (Netherlands)00385nz  a2200133n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400700006704000070013710000210014451000710016594200150023639708OI20260317121803.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1https://orcid.org/0009-0008-0997-23042ORCIDa0009-0008-0997-2304  aOI  aJauhiainen, Ilpo  aMetamusicX:Transdisciplinary Encounters in and Beyond Music926932  aPERSO_NAME00542nz  a2200157n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400700006704000070013710000180014451000710016251001120023355000240034594200150036939709OI20260317121804.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1https://orcid.org/0009-0002-2688-50302ORCIDa0009-0002-2688-5030  aOI  aReyes, Alicia  aMetamusicX:Transdisciplinary Encounters in and Beyond Music926932  aNon-anthropocentric composition: Decentralising the human through contemporary performance practices939710  aPosthumanism940150  aPERSO_NAME01926nz  a2200217n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711001050007436600270017950000370020650000320024351000870027552010770036255000710143955000570151055000710156790500550163894200150169339710OI20260130161236.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aNon-anthropocentric Composition: Decentralising the Human through Contemporary Performance Practices  k2024/09/01l2034/06/30  aAssis, Paulo de4Promoter916044  aAlicia Reyes4Fellow939709  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aThis doctoral proposal embarks on an artistic research project aimed at the development and refinement of my transdisciplinary compositional practice, with a distinct focus on exploring non-anthropocentric approaches to the creation of musical and multimedia works. The overarching objective is to transcend conventional paradigms that centre human beings as the main characters in musical performances, exploring alternative conceptualisations of artistic expression that prioritise interconnectedness with the broader ecological fabric. Grounded in ecological theories, biological imagination, and posthuman philosophies, this research seeks to develop new modes of art-making that engage with and reflect upon the agency of non-human entities and natural processes.  This research endeavour will contribute to the emergence of new compositional languages that challenge anthropocentric norms, offering a fresh perspective on the role of the composer/director in artistic collective processes and on the transformative potential of non-anthropocentric musical practices.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aEcology1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7150926417  aInterdisciplinarity1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q849359927077  a24114Principal fundingk2024/09/01l2034/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME01699nz  a2200205n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711001130007436600270018750000380021450000320025251000870028452009200037155000710129155000610136290500550142394200150147839711OI20260130161037.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aPrototyping a New Compositional Paradigm: The Augmented Audience and the Internet of Wearable Musical Things  k2024/09/01l2034/06/30  aImpett, Jonathan9163574Promoter  aChonglian Yu9397064Fellow  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aThis research explores speculative socio-technological trajectories to establish a new compositional paradigm, focusing on the concept of the augmented audience by prototyping wearables named the Internet of Wearable Musical Things (IoWMT). The study sets up a dual framework: a macro framework for conceptual and theoretical development and a micro framework for technical implementation and experimentation. This dual framework forms the basis of the research process, generating new compositional approaches. Together, they provide the context, techniques, and tools for the compositional practices. This research offers a reflective artistic perspective on our unpredictable technological future by converging fields, including computer music, human-computer interaction, speculative aesthetics, transhumanism, and gamification. It also conveys a personal attitude toward contemporary technological development.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aTechnology1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11016921633  a24114Principal fundingk2024/09/01l2034/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME00527nz  a2200157n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400750006704000070014210000280014994200150017750000620019255000520025455100630030639712OI20260330112619.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3202952WikidataaQ3202959David Pohle  aOI  aPohle, Davidd1624-1695  aPERSO_NAME  ihttp://wikidata.org/entity/P106697240aSchütz, Heinrich  aComposer93625ihttp://wikidata.org/entity/P106  aMerseburg (Germany)939862ihttp://wikidata.org/entity/P2002169nz  a2200193n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711000570007436600270013150000370015850000360019551000870023152015160031855000710183490500550190594200150196039713OI20260130160748.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aTerrestrial: Toward an Ecological Emergence in Music  k2024/09/01l2034/06/30  aAssis, Paulo de4Promoter916044  a Ilpo Jauhiainen4Fellow939708  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aThe research examines the potentiality of emergence and environmental intelligence as a compositional and performative tool. It focuses on a de/reterritorialization process that – to borrow from Deleuzian terminology – is becoming-environment of music and becoming-music of an environment. The key questions are: what is music and what could it become? How could environmentally sensitive and responsive music operate in the age of accelerated environmental change and ever-ubiquitous intelligent technology?  Drawing from Assemblage Theory, Hyperobjects, New Materialism and Posthumanism, the research seeks to formulate a new 21st century musical practice and aesthetic as that of a constructive agency between environment, music, technology and philosophy; to posit an idea of composer/performer as a facilitator – philosopher, gardener, catalyst – between contemporary society, science and sonic arts. The focus is on generative (self-organising and -evolving) musical practices that cover electroacoustic and contemporary classical music, new global musical styles as well as the emerging possibilities and combinations of music and sound art.  Alongside written theoretical work and a series of compositions and performances, the practice-based research consists of developing an AI application (‘Terrestrial’) that will act as an intelligent algorithmic tool between the composer/performer and a given environment (e.g. an ecosystem, hyperobject, network or assemblage) – actual or virtual.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  a24114Principal fundingk2024/09/01l2034/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME04084nz  a2200205n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711001240007436600270019850000330022550000340025851000870029252033420037955000710372166700160379290500550380894200150386339714OI20260130160439.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aDubrovnik Salons Reenacted: Rethinking Performative Concepts and the Rituals of Performing 18th- and 19th-Century Music  k2020/09/01l2030/06/30  aBeghin, Tom4Promoter917739  aJelača, Ivana4Fellow926640  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aInheriting Western art music values from the 19th century, modern concert halls have promoted a culture of contemplating music in silence (Small 1998). This is in stark contrast to the performing and listening practices of the 18th and early 19th century, when musical events were far more interactive and left room for spontaneous reactions and social contact among all the partakers. This kind of musical sociability was particularly present in the salons (Weber 1997; Melton 2001; Klorman 2019; Cypess 2022) in which music tended to be tailored to the performance ritual (Haynes/Burgess 2016; LeGuin 2007). While historically informed performance practices (HIP) evoke new ways of thinking about music, HIP musicians perform in largely anachronistic environments, accepting the modern concert hall protocol.  Within the emerging field of reenactment studies, which in its different forms and genres focuses on the (empirical) investigation of a specific historical culture or the historical consciousness, current performative strategies related to experimental theatre and performance art are strongly embraced. The 'performative turn',' i.e., the shift from the role of spectator not only to that of participant but also of co-player, is the prerequisite for experiencing community among all the partakers. In this respect, there is a strong inclination to role reversal and to the blurring of boundaries between art and non-art, real life and representation, the aesthetic and the political, etc. (McKenzie 2001; Fischer-Lichte 2009; Dolan 2010; Schechner 2013, Carlson 2018). While putting myself in multiple roles, equivalent to those of a salonnière (music curator, performer, producer) in order to reenact salon performances of the past within an experimental framework, I am using the cultural life of Dubrovnik, Croatia, as groundwork for my research. Armed with a substantial amount of extant materials and sources from archives, museums and libraries (musical scores, instruments, correspondence, treatises, and literary sources), I will trace the works that Dubrovnik salonnières of the past were performing, the instruments they could have used, the spaces where salon gatherings took place and these women’s relation to the local and European cultural circles of the time. Furthermore, Dubrovnik’s preserved architecture has already given rise to characteristic site-specific performative concepts during the past seventy years and continues to evoke manifold possibilities. Experiments with materials (assembling repertoires with crossing-over between modern and period instruments and multimedia) and environments (the use of historical venues as performance spaces and the engagement of audiences as co-players) will open up a dialogue between present and past in order to answer the following questions: How can we reenact (lost) historical practices in order to re-contextualize our established rituals of performing and listening? What are the possible roles of a 21st-century salonnière? How can she establish a new dynamism within the performance?  Organizing salon gatherings for the purpose of this research will take on two directions; one is open to the public as artistic reenacted performances; the other consists of workshops targeted to specific groups of audiences and focused on selected topics.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aNot present  a24114Principal fundingk2020/09/01l2030/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME00353cz  a2200145n  450000100060000000300040000600500170001000800410002704000080006810000320007640000370010840000300014540000170017594200150019239715DLC20250218105153.0801105n| azannaabn          |b aaa        aDLC1 aBernhardt, Sarahd1844-19231 aBernhardt, Rosine Bernard Damala1 aBernard, Henriette-Rosine0 aDivine Sarah  aPERSO_NAME00221nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000220007494200150009639716OI20250218111653.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aBockholdt, Ursula  aPERSO_NAME00217nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000180007494200150009239717OI20250218112107.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aThew, Lisbeth  aPERSO_NAME00518nz  a2200145n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400700006704000070013710000180014451000880016251001070025094200150035739718OI20260317121804.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1https://orcid.org/0009-0008-8626-48702ORCIDa0009-0008-8626-4870  aOI  aProehl, Blake  aDeclassifying the Classics: Rhetoric, Technology, and Performance, 1750-1850926931  aC. P. E. Bach, Silbermann, and Friederici: Materiality in Galant and Classical Clavichord Music939722  aPERSO_NAME00223nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000240007494200150009839719OI20250218113342.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aBrückner, Wolfgang  aPERSO_NAME00218nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000190007494200150009339720OI20250218113431.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aBlickle, Peter  aPERSO_NAME00218nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000190007494200150009339721OI20250218113513.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aBreuer, Dieter  aPERSO_NAME01927nz  a2200217n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711001000007436600270017450000330020150000390023451000870027352010780036055000710143855000610150955000690157090500550163994200150169439722OI20260130160116.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aC. P. E. Bach, Silbermann, and Friederici: Materiality in Galant and Classical Clavichord Music  k2024/09/01l2034/06/30  aBeghin, Tom4Promoter917739  aProehl, Blake4Collaborator939718  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aC. P. E. Bach’s fabled Silbermann clavichord is perhaps his best-known instrument, but another deserves consideration for its impact on his musical output: a clavichord by Friederici. At this newer instrument, Bach composed his nearly twenty years’ worth of Hamburg works. This clavichord updated Bach’s musical medium to the latest technologies, yet his older instrument was retained and still showcased to visitors. This research project seeks the significance of Bach’s Friederici clavichord and investigates how its affordances differed from those of the Silbermann. As I examine compositions by Bach and contemporaries, I will use enactment and embodiment to glean insights in Bach’s entanglements with his instruments and will engage in empirical measurements of clavichords to understand performer-instrument interaction. Deepening the understanding of the differences between two types of clavichords and their respective roles alongside the rising fortepiano will broaden the clavichord’s influence in our perception of Galant and Classical repertoire.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aClavichord1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q19182392126  aKeyboard music1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q105715630921412  a24114Principal fundingk2024/09/01l2034/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME00457nz  a2200145n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400820006704000070014910000330015694200150018955000500020455100570025439723OI20260330112619.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q25629432WikidataaQ25629439Werner Wolffheim  aOI  aWolffheim, Wernerd1877-1930  aPERSO_NAME  ihttp://wikidata.org/entity/P10693885aWriter  948ihttp://wikidata.org/entity/P19aBerlin (Germany)00366nz  a2200133n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400850006704000070015211000350015941000230019494200150021739724OI20260330112619.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q299666902WikidataaQ299666909Leo Liepmannssohn  aOI  aLeo Liepmannssohn, Antiquariat  aLiepmannssohn, Leo  aCORPO_NAME00387nz  a2200133n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400840006704000070015111000580015841000220021694200150023839725OI20260330112619.0250218|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q154319282WikidataaQ154319289Martin Breslauer  aOI  aMartin Breslauer, Verlagsbuchhändler und Antiquariat  aBreslauer, Martin  aCORPO_NAME00304nz  a2200121n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002602400760006704000070014315000170015094200150016739726OI20260330112619.0250218|| aca||babn           | a|a     d  1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2733832WikidataaQ2733839memento mori  aOI  aMemento mori  aTOPIC_TERM00488cz  a2200157n  450000100060000000300040000600500170001000800410002702400790006804000100014710000300015